5. Wear Comfortable Underwear

Comfortable underwear can help keep your sanitary pad securely positioned. Choose underwear that provides enough support without feeling too tight.

6. Dispose of Used Pads Properly

Wrap used sanitary pads before placing them in an appropriate waste bin. Do not flush them down the toilet.

Proper disposal is an important part of responsible menstrual hygiene.

7. Stay Hydrated

Drink enough water during your period. Staying hydrated supports general health and can help you feel better throughout the day.

8. Eat Nutritious Foods

Include fruits, vegetables, whole grains, protein, and iron-rich foods in your diet. This is particularly useful if you experience regular menstrual bleeding.

9. Track Your Period

Record the first day of your period, the number of bleeding days, your approximate flow, and any symptoms you experience.

Tracking your cycle can help you understand your usual pattern and recognise significant changes.

10. Know When to Seek Medical Advice

Severe pain, unusually heavy bleeding, persistent irregular periods, bleeding between periods, or symptoms such as dizziness and weakness should not be ignored.

A healthcare professional can help determine whether there is an underlying reason for these symptoms.

Make Period Hygiene Simple

Good menstrual hygiene is not about following complicated rules. It is about changing your menstrual product regularly, keeping your hands and body clean, choosing comfortable protection, and paying attention to your health.

